on  general milly said it was offensive to  suggest that members of the military  would be attempting to be quote woke by  merely reading about an academic theory  what is your thought on on the the  tension and discussion over  what the military is doing especially  because they they recently  have been investigating according to  secretary austin extremism within the  military  i don't think it's appropriate um i  don't think we should be doing it  but it tells you uh how far down this  road we've already gone  and how there is a need to push back uh  before we go  any further um this is a very divisive  concept it is playing one american  against another  um and and and that just will end it  will end badly  and and so um you know when when when  president trump um made an effort  to uh to stop this trend um  i think he was on the right track there  and uh  president biden seems intent on you know  doing the opposite of everything  that president trump did uh regardless  of of the merits of what the  the former president was doing but in  some cases  donald trump had it right and and uh i  think this is one of them  jason reilly fox news contributor wall  street journal uh  columnist and senior fellow at the  manhattan institute thank you so much  for your time  thank you  this is geraldo rivera with your fox  news commentary  coming up former president trump goes to  texas today  there is no more dire threat today than  the crisis  on our southern border saying at his  weekend rally in ohio  just a few short months ago drugs were  way down  human trafficking was way down it was  all way down  now after four months in a row of a  hundred thousand plus migrants being  apprehended  he blames president biden undoing some  trump border policies which  the new administration says were  inhumane vice president harris went to  texas last friday  welcome to el paso welcome to my  community  to the new ellis island to the capital  of the border  democratic congresswoman veronica  escobar greeted harris who republicans  demanded for months go to the border  then were critical she avoided areas  where the migrant surge is most intense  for the vice president counters  it is here in el paso that the previous  administration's  child separation policy was unveiled  and el paso where the return to mexico  policy from the previous administration  was implemented and harris insists we  must address the root causes of why they  come  we're talking about stories that involve  horrendous  tales of abuse  and fear and harm a former president  trump will be joined  by republican governor greg abbott in  westlake near  mcallen these are the areas that  republicans especially have said and  the numbers prove it these are the high  traffic areas  we caught up with fox business network's  grady trimble in la jolla texas outside  mcallen  when the vice president visited last  week they felt like  she didn't go to the right areas because  she didn't go to  the rio grande valley where migrants are  moving through here  so they've been critical of that and i  don't think it's an accident that  they're coming to this area they're  doing it intentionally because  this is where so many of the problems  are right now  so you're down there what have you seen  so far we were reporting live along the  border wall and it's interesting to see  in this area because  this is uh one of the areas where  construction was halted by the bina  administration so you can see areas  where the walls constructed  and then you can see areas where  obviously there were plans for wall  and and it's not there at all and then  after that we headed over  that was near mission texas we headed  over to la jolla which is about 20  minutes down the road  and this is one of the areas where we  are now where a lot of migrants and  families have been moving through  and that's exactly what we saw this  morning about  two dozen or so maybe more including  women  children and they were essentially  corralled by border patrol loaded into  buses  and presumably from there they go to the  processing facilities elsewhere  all right now describe the terrain  because  nobody can see anything where you are  right now what's it like  at the exact border spot where they're  coming across  so it's we are i would say a half a mile  to a mile  from the actual border this is an area  where if we  went through to the actual border and to  the rio grande river  uh we would have to go through very  dense brush  we would have to go over private  property uh and  eventually we would make it to the where  the the actual border is  so by the time they get to this area  they've gone through  all of that and that's when they  encounter border patrol potentially for  the first time  on the ground but there are of course  coast guard and other agencies border  patrol  on the river going up and down the river  and they're also  uh circling in helicopters and trying to  survey by  air to see these groups of migrants move  through this area  and then the ground crews meet them  where we are right now  do most try to cross the river at night  i was talking with the texas department  of public safety today and they're  essentially down here to assist  border patrol because border patrol is  so overwhelmed  with processing so they need help on the  border itself  and i asked that question as well and  they said it depends on the weather  it depends on the types of groups  whether  it's you know people with young kids  whether it's  people who appear to be members of  cartels  or smugglers um so you will see people  uh traveling in the early morning hours  just before the sun comes up  but when we saw that group of a couple  dozen people  it was broad daylight it was probably  nine in the morning  did any of them run did they were they  just walking together and then they were  just apprehended  what happened so my understanding is  actually that some of them  want to be apprehended because then  they're in the united states and sent to  a processing facility in the united  states and  you know it's it's as crazy as it sounds  a bit of respite after such a  treacherous journey that's the families  and the people who are  you know coming here from central  america on the flip side you have  people who are from mexico in fact griff  jenkins who's also down here with us  captured video of about five men who who  identified themselves  to him as people who were from mexico  they weren't with one of these groups of  women and children and families  and they did try to run from border  patrol so you have  both uh going on at the same time and  you know  border patrol and the other agencies  that are down here  are you know are constantly dealing with  with both of those  things those who are apprehended do they  all stay do some get sent back  i think some get sent back but honestly  when when we're talking to the  people on the ground near the border  they don't really know what  happens once they're loaded up into the  buses and taken to  the processing facility so they're not  as familiar with that  side of things what they are very  familiar with  is them actually being apprehended and  you know looking at the numbers  it is staggering just in the rio grande  valley where we are  2100 apprehensions in the last 24 hours  that doesn't even include the other  stretches of the southern border  elsewhere in texas and elsewhere in the  country  so those numbers are staggering and  that's what they're dealing with on the  ground here not necessarily the  processing side  um which is you know in a totally  different area  something else i know that you are  looking into while you're  at the border while you're reporting on  all this is  the drug smuggling and that's a big part  of the story at the border as well  it is and you can't even separate the  two  parts of this which is people coming  across the border and drugs coming  across the border because when we talk  to uh people on the ground here in law  enforcement  they tell us you know we're seeing tens  of thousands of pounds of drugs come  through  in a month's time and oftentimes the  cartels  are the ones leading the migrants  through here and they're getting paid to  do that  by the the migrants who are you know  seeking a better life here  but the the law enforcement that we've  talked to say  that's a problem because they're  literally receiving millions of dollars  to do this  and it's making the cartels more and  more powerful  and more and more dangerous in fact one  of the things we're following is  operation lone star  and that is essentially uh commissioned  by the governor in texas greg  abbott who said you know border patrol  so overwhelmed we're going to send some  of our people with the department of  public safety  to get involved as well and they found  over a million dollars in cash 140  firearms  again you know thousands of pounds of  illegal drugs  in uh just the few months that that  operation has been going on  right and we're talking about a variety  of drugs coming across right  oh yeah there's there's marijuana  there's cocaine and methamphetamine  those are the two largest categories of  types of drugs brought across  but there's also fentanyl too and as you  know  a lot of people across the country will  know that's extremely dangerous  and one of the reasons the you know  opioid epidemic has taken its toll on  people  in this country when you talk to a  border agent i know you did a short time  before we  we had our conversation do you feel  like they're overwhelmed or do they feel  like they have a handle on it  what are they saying i i think they feel  overwhelmed  or else they wouldn't be asking for help  from other state agencies whether it's  the  public safety department here in texas  or agencies  uh as far away as nebraska and florida  being sent  in to help um i i think that any amount  of help they can receive  they